---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const struck = assertion('what-the-tornado-struck')
const injuries = assertion('two-injuries-named-and-thirty-counted')
const clock = assertion('the-paper-and-the-file-disagree-about-the-clock')
const money = assertion('the-million-dollars-that-were-not-apart')

const second = assertion('a-second-storm-the-file-does-not-hold')
const track = assertion('five-miles-in-a-semicircle-around-bluffton')
const roads = assertion('twenty-four-hours-to-open-the-roads')
const woodlots = assertion('what-a-gauge-cannot-record')

const basins = assertion('one-storm-two-basins')
const main = assertion('ice-broke-the-village-water-main')
const pumped = assertion('twelve-houses-pumped-out-in-near-zero-weather')
const quarry = assertion('the-creek-and-the-quarry-at-one-level')
const day = assertion('the-creek-rose-a-day-before-the-river')

const moneySpec = bar(
  money.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  money.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'The band the file recorded, dollars of 1950', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="what-the-paper-saw">
  <p>
    Two of the events in this county's record were put there by machines. A row in a federal storm
    file made a tornado; a river gauge reading of 7,740 cubic feet a second made a flood. Both
    carried the same admission in different words — this is a number and it does not know what it
    did. The county's own weekly newspaper answers both, and disagrees with the machines about the
    clock and about how many storms there were. It seemed to disagree about the cost too, until the
    file's damage column turned out to be a band the paper's figure sits inside.
  </p>

  <h2>What the tornado struck</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={struck} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={injuries} open />
  <p>
    Two named injuries against thirty counted is not a contradiction. The paper is published in
    Bluffton, at the far end of a track that started on the south-east side of Lima, and it reports
    the end of the storm because that is where its subscribers were. Lima's casualties are in
    nobody's account that survives here, because none of the city's sixty-six newspaper titles was
    ever digitized.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={clock} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={money} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="money"
    title="The band the federal file actually recorded"
    caption="The file's $2.5M is not an estimate: it is damage category 6 of nine, $500,000 to $5,000,000, printed as a dollar amount. The paper's one and a half million falls inside it. This page said for a day that the two were a million apart."
    columns={['Bound', 'Property damage']}
    rows={money.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `$${f.value.toLocaleString()}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="money" set:html={JSON.stringify(moneySpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>The storm that is in no file</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={second} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={track} />
  <p>
    An hour of separation and a different bearing fit two storms, and they fit one storm touching
    down twice. The paper's own word is "apparently". What is not in doubt is that a damaging wind
    crossed the county's northern edge that evening and left barns down around Beaverdam, and that
    the file which opens this county's weather record with a single tornado has no row for it.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={roads} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={woodlots} open />
  <p>
    That is the half of an event no instrument will ever hold: trees two feet through twisted off,
    the tops taken out of everything left standing, and dead rabbits and pheasants underneath
    because the storm came up too fast for anything to reach shelter. A file can tell you an F3
    tornado ran 9.7 miles. It cannot tell you a woodlot will be a mass of debris for months.
  </p>

  <h2>One storm, two basins</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={basins} open />
  <p>
    This is the trap. The account is in exactly the right week, it is vivid, it is local, and it is
    about a different river. Bluffton sits on Big Riley Creek, which
    drains north to the Blanchard; the gauge that recorded the largest flow ever measured on the
    Ottawa is twenty miles south. Same rain, same two days, different water — and the basin with
    the description had no instrument in it while the basin with the instrument left no
    description.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={main} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={pumped} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={quarry} />
  <p>
    Ice six to eight inches thick came down the creek and hit the bridges, in the paper's phrase,
    with the impact of trucks. It broke the water main at the Main street bridge, and the valve that
    should have isolated it was corroded shut, so the village had to close a second valve much
    further back and take a large part of Main street off the water with it. Then the temperature
    dropped to near zero, and the crews spent the night pumping basements so that furnaces could be
    lit.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={day} open />
  <p>
    The creek was a Wednesday and the river was a Thursday, which is what a small watershed and a
    large one do with the same rain. Neither source knows about the other. Putting them beside each
    other is the whole of what a corpus is for, and keeping them apart — not letting four feet of
    water in a lumber yard become a description of the Ottawa — is the rest of it.
  </p>
</Article>
